[0.018, "o", "\u001b[?2026h\r\u001b[32A  hardware PWM (100% duty for lower segments, proportional PWM duty on the active segment, and 0% duty for upper\u001b[K\r\n  segments).\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[?5W\u001b[J\n  Next concern — \u001b[1minteractive NSH command interface\u001b[m:\r\n\n  When a user connects to the USB-C NSH console while the bar graph daemon is already running in the background, what\r\n  commands or diagnostics should they be able to run from the NSH prompt (for example, \u001b[94mbargraph status\u001b[m to inspect\r\n  current ADC/PWM readings, \u001b[94mbargraph stop\u001b[m/\u001b[94mstart\u001b[m, or live stream/test modes), or does the app only need to run as a\r\n  background daemon without interactive subcommands?\r\n\n\u001b[90m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[34;1m> bargraph start, stop, and status all sound like good commands to have. Any other options?\u001b[m\r\n\n\u001b[37m▸ Thought for 6s, 146 tokens\u001b[m\r\n\n  Beyond \u001b[94mstart\u001b[m, \u001b[94mstop\u001b[m, an"]
[0.000, "o", "d \u001b[94mstatus\u001b[m, two subcommands have practical value during breadboard bring-up, while others add\r\n  complexity without clear payoff:\r\n\n  • \u001b[94mbargraph test\u001b[39;1m (Hardware self-test sweep)\u001b[m: Sweeps the bar graph smoothly from 0% to 100% and back once\r\n  (temporarily pausing ADC polling if the daemon is running). This isolates LED wiring and PWM slice/channel\r\n  configuration from ADC/potentiometer wiring bugs during initial breadboard bring-up.\r\n  • \u001b[94mbargraph set <0..100>\u001b[39;1m (Manual override)\u001b[m: Pauses ADC polling and locks the bar graph to a fixed percentage so you\r\n  can verify a specific segment's PWM brightness or measure current draw with a multimeter.\r\n\n  By contrast, subcommands like runtime pin remapping, live continuous terminal plotting (\u001b[94mmonitor\u001b[m), or runtime filter\r\n  tuning add state synchronization overhead and terminal spam without solving a real problem for a 6-LED breadboard\r\n  target.\r\n\n  Would you like to include \u001b[94mtest\u001b[m and/or \u001b[94mset\u001b[m alongside \u001b[94mstar"]
[0.000, "o", "t\u001b[m, \u001b[94mstop\u001b[m, and \u001b[94mstatus\u001b[m, or keep the CLI strictly to \u001b[94mstart\u001b[m,\r\n  \u001b[94mstop\u001b[m, and \u001b[94mstatus\u001b[m?\r\n\n\u001b[90m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[34;1m> add test and set. Is there a path out of setting the bargraph back to sensing the pot's value?\u001b[m\r\n⣯  \u001b[90mWorking.\u001b[94m..\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[90m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[94m>\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[90m──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"]
[0.000, "o", "──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[90mesc to cancel\u001b[m\u001b[77X\u001b[77C\u001b[33mauto\u001b[39;2m · Gemini 4 Argon · medium\r\u001b[m\u001b[2A\u001b[2C\u001b[?2026l"]
[0.101, "o", "\u001b[?2026h\r\u001b[2A⣟  \u001b[90mWorking..\u001b[m\n\n\u001b[10D\u001b[?2026l"]
